Z||000000||87GTBro||06-23-2001||01:38 AM||||OK...  Please someone tell me they can help me!  First, here's my story:<P>I have replaced both rear calipers and rotors on my 86 GT because of a nasty mess it made last week.  The right rear caliper froze and caught fire and busted a brake line and it just has me all pissed off right about now...<P>OK... so I had the car in the garage... I do all the work myself because I don't trust anybody else.  I replaced the brake lines, rotors and calipers.  I am having problems with my ebrake!  The passenger side ebrake works fine.  The problem I am having is on the drivers side.  I have played with the lever on the caliper and it is operating in the opposite direction than it should be.  Normally, the ebrake cable pulls back on the lever towards the rear of the car and this action pushes the piston out and clamps the rotor.  Well, it seems this new caliper they gave me at the auto parts store is different!  The piston pushes out when the lever is pushed toward the front of the car.<P>Was there a recall that replaced everything and made it work that way?  I am completely lost!!!  Either my car has never been recalled or the caliper I bought was assembled with the wrong actuator screw or something!  Is there a website where I can check to see if all the recalls have been done on my Fiero?<P>I am $400 in the hole on this little fiasco and I am still not able to drive my baby!!!  I just might hurt someone!!  Or myself!!!  HELP!!<P>Oh!  and if someone has a digital camera and an 86 GT... can you take pictures of the calipers and e-brake levers for me to see how they are supposed to look?  The haynes manual shows something completely different than what was on my car!  Thanks!||||reg||8
Z||000001||Oreif||06-23-2001||01:41 AM||||It is the wrong caliper. Are you sure they didn't give you the wrong side? When I replaced mine I bought them from NAPA and they worked like the originals.||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTBro||06-23-2001||01:46 AM||||It is the right caliper... both sides have the bleeder valve on the top and the connection for the brake line on the top... so it has to be the right caliper for that side right???  or else the bleeder valve and connection would be on the bottom???||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fierowrecker||06-23-2001||01:53 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 87GTBro:<BR><B>It is the right caliper... both sides have the bleeder valve on the top and the connection for the brake line on the top... so it has to be the right caliper for that side right???  or else the bleeder valve and connection would be on the bottom???</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Correct caliper, wrong guts...<BR>Get it exchanged...<BR>Just because it is NAPA, doesn't mean they don't have misteakes...<BR>I had the wrong caliper in the right box from NAPA...<BR>crash...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||87GTBro||06-23-2001||01:58 AM||||See... that's what I thought... It is the right caliper for the job, but they must have accidently put the actuator screw from the other caliper in there instead!  Does that sound possible?  Would I be correct in saying that?  They're gonna think I'm retarded when I take it back!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||06-23-2001||01:06 PM||||Nope, that sounds like exactly what happened. I'v too have had trouble with NAPA brakes being packaged wrong. I bought some rebuilt calipers for my old Bronco, and they gave me one correct one, and one that fit some Chevy truck of some kind. I would just take it back and tell them their ebrake system is assembled wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000006||87GTBro||06-23-2001||08:57 PM||||Good news...  The caliper was assembled wrong.  It was the right caliper for that side, but the cylinder and actuator screw were supposed to be for the other one.<P>Someone must have been a little tipsy on the job!<P>I didn't get those calipers from NAPA.  I got em from a place called Burton Auto Supply.  And I can say this... I will never get parts there again!!!  I took the caliper back and they gave me my money and the core charge back (cause they didn't have my old caliper anymore)  The only other place that had the caliper around was (in fact)  NAPA.  They had to ship it in from a town 40 miles away, but I got it in the same day.  Only thing is I wound up paying $207 for one caliper!!!  The caliper cost was $70 (unloaded) and the core charge was $94!!!!  Can you say !@%^!#$!???  <P>So if any of you are reading this and have a caliper from a parts car you wanna sell me cheap I will pay the s/h and whatever you ask... (as long as it is less than $94 of course...)  I want my core charge back!!!<P>I don't have the car on the road quite yet...  I am finding that it is very difficult to bleed the brakes by yourself!!!  AH!!!  Someone want to come over and help?||||reg||6
Z||000007||lowCG||06-24-2001||11:55 PM||||Uh,that's a long drive for me,but congrats on getting it all figured out,am familiar with bad work,and it doesn't seem to matter how much you pay either.||||reg||6
Z||000008||Firefly||06-25-2001||01:24 PM||||have have this caliper that was kicking around in a trunk of a fiero, have to look at it when I get home and see if it is a Fiero part and what side.||||reg||1
